  "response": "Here's a Python function called `format_currency(n)` that formats a numeric value as currency with:\n\n- A `$` symbol at the beginning\n- Commas separating every three digits\n- A minus sign before the dollar sign for negative numbers (e.g., `-1234567.89`)\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Formats a number as currency with dollar signs and comma separators.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int or float): The numeric value to format.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: Formatted string such as '$4,640,039' or '-$123.45'.\n    \"\"\"\n    sign = '-' if n < 0 else ''\n    num_str = f\"{abs(int(n)):.2f}\"  # Convert to string with up to 2 decimal places\n    \n    # Insert commas every 3 digits from the right\n    def add_commas(s):\n        # Skip first part, group rest in chunks of 3 from end\n        idx = len(s) % 3\n        return s[idx:] + (',' + s[:idx]) if idx else s\n\n    formatted = f\"{sign}$\"\n    formatted += add_commas(num_str.replace('.', ''))  # Remove decimal part and add commas\n    if '.' in str(int(n)):  # Include decimal part only if present\n        return formatted[:-3] + formatted[-3] + '.' + num_str[num_str.index('.'): ]\n\n    # Corrected final approach using a cleaner method\n```\n\nActually, here's a more reliable and cleaner version of the function:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Formats a number as currency with dollar signs and comma separators.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int or float): The numeric value to format.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: Formatted string such as '$4,640,039' or '-$123.45'.\n    \"\"\"\n    import locale\n    loc_str = locale.format_string('%.2f', abs(n), grouping=True)\n    \n    if n < 0:\n        return '-' + loc_str\n    else:\n        return '$' + loc_str\n```\n\nHowever, if `locale` isn't desired or available, here's a robust and portable alternative that avoids it:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Formats a number as currency with dollar signs and comma separators.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int or float): The numeric value to format.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: Formatted string such as '$4,640,039' or '-$123.45'.\n    \"\"\"\n    sign = '-' if n < 0 else ''\n    abs_n = abs(n)\n    \n    integer_part = int(abs_n)\n    decimal_part = f\".{int(abs_n * 100) % 100:02d}\" if isinstance(abs_n, float) and '.' in str(abs_n) else \"\"\n    \n    num_str = f\"{integer_part:,}\"\n\n    formatted = f\"{sign}${num_str}\"\n    \n    return formatted + decimal_part\n```\n\n✅ This version avoids locale dependency, properly handles floats with decimals, and matches the required format for integers and negative values alike.",
